  "text": "Certainly! Here's a Python function that meets your requirements. It:\n\n- Converts the input string to lowercase.\n- Removes punctuation.\n- Splits the string into words.\n- Builds a dictionary where each key is a word and the value is its count.\n\n### \u2705 Solution Code:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation using string.punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split the text into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Create the histogram dictionary\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d Explanation:\n\n- `text.lower()` makes everything lowercase to ensure case-insensitivity.\n- `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` creates a translation table to remove all punctuation.\n- `text.translate(...)` removes punctuation from the text.\n- `text.split()` splits the string into words by whitespace.\n- A dictionary is used to count the occurrences of each word.\n\n### \u2705 Unit Tests:\n\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nLet me know if you want to support additional cases (e.g., handling hyphenated words, or apostrophes like \"don't\").",
